  "practicalInfo": {
    "bestTimeToVisit": "April to October is Bali's dry season — the easiest weather with kids, with sunny days and the calmest seas in Sanur and Nusa Dua. May, June and September are quieter than the July–August peak.",
    "gettingThere": "Fly into Bali's Ngurah Rai International Airport (DPS). It is about 15–30 minutes to Sanur or Nusa Dua and 60–90 minutes to Ubud, depending on traffic.",
    "gettingAround": "Hire a private driver (around Rp 700,000–900,000 / $45–$60 per day) with child seats on request — the easiest option with children. Use Grab and Gojek for short hops; avoid scooters with young kids.",
    "currency": "The Indonesian rupiah (IDR). Cards work in hotels and larger restaurants; carry cash for warungs, markets and small entry fees. ATMs are common in tourist areas.",
    "simCard": "Buy an Indonesia eSIM before you fly so maps, ride apps and messaging work on arrival — essential when coordinating a family day out.",
    "visa": "Most nationalities get the e-VoA for Rp 500,000 (about $35), valid 30 days. Bali also charges a Rp 150,000 (about $10) tourist levy per person, including children. Pay both online before you fly."
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "question": "Is Bali safe for travelling with kids?",
      "answer": "Yes — Bali is very family-friendly and used to children. Take normal precautions: sun protection, hydration, mosquito repellent, and drink only bottled or filtered water to avoid stomach upsets. The south has good private hospitals (BIMC and Siloam) if you need them."
    },
    {
      "question": "What's the best area to stay in Bali with young children?",
      "answer": "Sanur and Nusa Dua are the easiest — calm, shallow, reef-protected beaches and family resorts. Ubud is great for nature if you pick a stay with a pool. Skip the busy nightlife strips of central Kuta and Canggu with little ones."
    },
    {
      "question": "How much do Bali Safari and Waterbom cost?",
      "answer": "Waterbom Bali international tickets are about Rp 495,000 for adults and Rp 355,000 for children aged 2–11, with a family pass (2 adults + 2 children) around Rp 1,581,000. Bali Safari and Marine Park starts near $38 per person. Booking online is usually cheaper."
    },
    {
      "question": "Do children need a visa and pay the Bali tourist levy?",
      "answer": "Yes to both. The Indonesia e-VoA is Rp 500,000 (about $35) per person including children, valid 30 days. Bali's tourist levy is a one-time Rp 150,000 (about $10) per person, also including children. Pay both online before you fly."
    },
    {
      "question": "How do we get around Bali with kids?",
      "answer": "A private driver is easiest — around Rp 700,000–900,000 a day, with child seats if you request them ahead. The Grab and Gojek apps cover short trips cheaply. Avoid renting scooters with young children given Bali's busy traffic."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is 7 days enough for a family trip to Bali?",
      "answer": "Yes. A week lets you settle into two relaxed bases — the southern beaches plus Ubud — covering wildlife, a waterpark, gentle nature and plenty of pool time without over-driving. Save island-hopping to Nusa Penida or the Gilis for a longer trip when children are older."
    }
  ],
